      "math_academy_id": "e-477",
      "problem": "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-\\sin^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad  \\cos^2 x = 1-\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
